Oral Candidasis
Palliative & End of Life
Adherent white oral plaques, Erythematous underlying mucosa, Oral burning sensation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-041
Reason for Visit: Acute oral pain and absolute refusal to swallow essential palliative medications or fluids due to severe mucosal burning.
Location: Residential Aged Care Facility
Present at Visit: Patient, Registered Nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the patient has developed sudden, severe oral pain over the last 24 hours, leading to a complete refusal of all oral intake, including critical scheduled opioid analgesia. Urgent assessment was strictly required to diagnose the acute mucosal pathology, provide immediate topical/systemic symptom relief, and prevent an impending systemic pain crisis and severe dehydration prior to the next regular in-hours clinical review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My mouth is completely on fire. It feels raw and tastes like metal. I absolutely cannot swallow those pain pills."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 48 hours, the patient developed prominent, distinct, creamy white and distinctly yellowish plaques strictly adhering to the buccal mucosa, palate, and tongue. Erythematous, raw, and highly sensitive underlying mucosal tissue is definitively exposed when the superficial plaques are manually scraped off by the tongue. The patient reports a severe, continuous burning sensation and distinct localized pain within the entire oral cavity. Pronounced odynophagia strictly occurring when swallowing liquids or pills indicates contiguous extension directly into the esophagus. The patient describes a noticeable, severely altered sense of taste frequently described distinctly as metallic or persistently bitter. Angular cheilitis is presenting as painful, erythematous fissuring explicitly strictly located at the commissures of the lips. The patient has a smooth, completely depapillated, and severely erythematous tongue distinctly characteristic of the atrophic clinical variant. There is a history of profound immunosuppression and continuous systemic corticosteroid administration for underlying disease management.
Functional Status: Conscious but acutely distressed by oral pain; refusing all oral intake.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute medication withdrawal and systemic pain crisis due to inability to swallow baseline analgesia.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is visibly uncomfortable, holding their jaw, reluctant to open their mouth or speak loudly.
Physical Exam: Oral examination reveals extensive, adherent white plaques across the buccal mucosa and hard palate. Gentle scraping with a tongue depressor reveals vividly erythematous, bleeding underlying mucosa. The tongue is beefy red, depapillated, and coated in thick plaques. Deep erythematous fissures are present at bilateral oral commissures. The diagnostic tetrad is fulfilled by the observed adherent white oral plaques, the explicitly erythematous underlying mucosa, and the patient's reported severe oral burning sensation.
Environmental Observations: A full cup of water and a cup of unadministered oral medications sit untouched on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient has missed two consecutive doses of sustained-release oral morphine due to severe odynophagia.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Oral Candidasis - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Odynophagia)
Differentials:

Viral Stomatitis (Herpes Simplex) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fluid-filled vesicles or distinct, punched-out ulcerations on the lips or labial mucosa; the presentation is classic for fungal plaques.

Chemotherapy-induced Mucositis - Ruled out/unlikely as the patient is not currently receiving active cytotoxic chemotherapy known to cause acute mucosal sloughing.

Aphthous Ulcers - Ruled out/unlikely due to the diffuse, generalized burning and widespread plaque formation, rather than discrete, localized, solitary crateriform ulcers with erythematous halos.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute crisis of severe oral candidiasis, heavily complicated by esophageal extension causing profound odynophagia. The objective evidence of widespread fungal plaques and raw mucosa directly explains the absolute refusal of oral intake. Because the patient is entirely unable to swallow their essential baseline opioid analgesia, delaying medical assessment until standard hours would directly precipitate a severe, systemic pain crisis and withdrawal syndrome. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was absolutely critical to bypass the oral route with parenteral analgesia tonight, and to prescribe rapidly acting systemic and topical antifungal therapies to restore oral function.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat subcutaneous morphine to provide immediate systemic pain relief and cover the missed oral doses. Prescribed oral nystatin drops (instructed to swish and swallow) and topical miconazole gel for the angular cheilitis. Prescribed a short course of oral fluconazole to target the suspected esophageal extension.
Positive Behavioral Change: When it is time to take your oral liquid medication (trigger), explicitly coat your mouth with the prescribed numbing gel 10 minutes prior (behavior). This temporarily blocks the pain receptors in the raw tissue, ensuring you can swallow the critical medications and fluids without triggering the severe burning sensation (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Continued facility nursing care with strict instructions for 2-hourly gentle mouth care.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provide ultra-soft sponge swabs for oral care; strictly avoid hard-bristled toothbrushes until the mucosa heals.
Follow-up: Facility GP review in 48 hours to assess response to antifungal therapy and ability to resume solid oral medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient remains completely unable to swallow even small sips of water after 24 hours of treatment, develops a high fever, or if systemic pain becomes uncontrolled despite the subcutaneous breakthrough medication, the nursing staff must contact the after-hours medical service immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The fungal infection is an opportunistic complication of the required steroid medications and underlying immunosuppression; it requires aggressive, targeted treatment.
Clinical Course: The acute burning pain should begin to subside within 48-72 hours of initiating systemic and topical antifungal therapy, allowing a gradual return to normal oral intake.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Nursing Staff: Do not attempt to force the patient to swallow solid pills. Crush essential medications and mix with a small amount of non-acidic puree (e.g., plain yogurt) once the numbing gel has taken effect, or utilize subcutaneous alternatives.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The steroids you take have weakened the local immune defenses in your mouth, allowing yeast to overgrow and burn the tissue. The white patches are the yeast, and the red tissue underneath is raw and inflamed. We have given you an injection for your body pain tonight. The drops and gels we prescribed will kill the yeast, and the numbing gel will allow you to swallow fluids without agony.
Actionable Steps: Use the numbing gel before attempting to eat or drink. Avoid all acidic, spicy, or hot foods. Swish the fungal drops around your mouth for as long as possible before swallowing.

SMS INFORMATION

